Chancellor Angela Merkel and President  Barack Obama regard each other as allies  even as friends the two countries share  close economic political and cultural  ties but there are differences when it  comes to some key issues the two  countries diverge many Germans lost a  great deal of trust in the u.s. since  news of the NSA scandal broke for years  US intelligence spied on German citizens  and companies the surveillance extended  to members of the government and even  the Chancellor's cellphone  many Germans regard Edward Snowden as a  hero but for a fair number of Americans  he's a traitor Germans are very  concerned about data privacy and  security organizations such as camp Act  campaign against mass surveillance here  in Germany there's a tradition of  criticism of political and economic  power which also derives from our  experiences under two dictatorships the  progressive element of German society at  least identifies with a general culture  of resistance and this element says  citizens must remain vigilant Americans  by contrast tend to worry less about  data privacy many American expats regard  the German passion for privacy with some  dim use meant it is a little bit over  the top for me to say okay we're so  concerned about privacy and I don't want  my name or the face of my child or  anything shown anywhere in a digital  sort of way that for me is too much and  I think we could all maybe stand to be a  little bit more in the middle on that  topic another sticking point is TTIP the  planned transatlantic free trade  agreement the agreement would give  corporations more rights and many  Germans worried that would come at the  expense of consumer interests US  companies would have an easier time  obtaining product approval in the EU  American companies don't understand the  protests  konista home the aim isn't to lower  standards it's to set common standards  which will allow us to offer consumers  and users better products more products  and more interesting products but  standards in the US are often lower than  in Germany food safety is one example we  want to issue our own food regulations  and decide whether we want to allow  genetically modified foods or not we  want to make those decisions on our own  and dirty of corporate lobbyists as few  rights as possible in today's globalized  economy US products and brands are  everywhere in Germany but that doesn't  mean Germany is ready to give up its own  identity
 

